The archive drifted silently above the desert, suspended by cables so thin they vanished against the sky. Inside, shelves of glass cylinders preserved fragments of forgotten languages—syllables etched in light, verbs folded into metallic petals. A caretaker moved between them with measured steps, recording the faint hum each fragment emitted at dusk.

No one remembered who built the structure. Some believed it was a failed observatory; others said it was a library designed for a future that never arrived. Every evening, the caretaker selected one cylinder at random and released its contents into the air. The words would shimmer briefly, rearrange themselves into a sentence no one had heard before, and dissolve like heat over sand.

Below, travelers sometimes paused, certain they had almost understood something important.
